sentences.l:
“Atreus’-son, most glorious, lord of men, Agamemnon,
not, surely, for Hector all his thoughts counsellor Zeus
will bring to pass, as many as he now perhaps hopes for;

for often he slackens and is unwilling to toil,
neither yielding to hesitation nor to follies of mind,
but looking to me and awaiting my impulse.

First, then, Odysseus, the equal of Zeus in cunning,
from sleep woke the Gerenian horseman Nestor,
calling out;

and for them the spears
upright on the butt-spike had been driven, and far off the bronze
shone like the lightning of father Zeus;

sentences.t:
οὔ νυ καὶ ἄλλοι ἔασι νεώτεροι υἷες Ἀχαιῶν
οἵ κεν ἔπειτα ἕκαστον ἐγείρειαν βασιλήων
πάντῃ ἐποιχόμενοι;

sentences.tr:
ou ny kai alloi easi neōteroi huies Achaiōn
hoi ken epeita hekaston egeireian basilēōn
pantēi epoichomenoi;

sentences.l:
